     It's been years since I read that guide, and I probably
didn't pay much attention to its details at the time anyway.
It ought to have included steps to update the appropriate
Makefile so that 'make' could do all the grunge work, but
that would have included a bunch of platform-dependent
variations so was probably rejected as too complicated to
write up.

     If you're doing things manually, there are two main
things to do (besides modifying dungeon.def and processing
it with both makedefs.exe and dgn_comp.exe, which you
seem to have managed if nethack is now looking for a new
special level).  The first thing is to run lev_comp to
build yourlevel.lev from the yourlevel.des you've made.
The second thing is to put the result where nethack can
find it.

     That second step has two alternatives, depending
upon your nethack configuration.  If you're using DLB to
collect all the read-only data files into one big file
(which you probably are if that guide's instructions
aren't working), you need to run dlb.exe to recreate the
"data library" file, which I think is called nhdat for
your platform. There's a file in the dat subdirectory
containing a list of nhdat's contents (or more
specifically, that you feed to dlb to tell it what to
put into the file it's constructing), and you'll need to
add yourlevel.lev to that.  Once it has the right list,
the dlb invocation (for Windows, from within the dat
subdirectory) is something like
  ..\util\dlb.exe -cfI nhdat nh.lis
(that's from memory and may have some details wrong).
The -c option is to create a new output file (and I don't
think the alternative of updating an existing file has
ever been implemented, since creating from scratch is
sufficient for nethack's needs); nhdat is the output file
name for the -f option; nh.lis is the name of a file
holding the contents list for the -I option (I'm not sure
that's the right name, but something like it should be
present in your dat directory if dlb was used to produce
the existing setup).  Once you've created nhdat with new
extended contents, copy it to the playground directory.
You'll probably want to first save the one that's already
there in case you need to revert to it at some point.

     If you're not using the DLB config, then instead of
creating and copying a new nhdat file you just need to copy
the new yourlevel.lev file to the playground directory.  I
suspect that the guide you're using assumes this config,
based on another thread here a couple of months back.  That
user changed config.h to disable DLB.  I would do the dlb
updating instead, but you'll need to reconstruct nhdat (and
copy it to the playground) each time you tweak your level.
Disabling DLB may make more sense if you expect to do that
a lot, since then you could just copy lev_comp's output to
the playground directory.  Using 'make' renders this moot,
since it will do all intermediary steps--once the Makefile
has the right incantation to tell it what those are.


